Lamellar coarsening in Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) and its influence on room temperature tensile behavior

Ian Baker

Corresponding author at: 14 Engineering Dr, Dartmouth College, Hanover, NH 03755, USA. Tel: +1 603 646 2184.

Fanling Meng

###### Abstract

As-cast nanostructured eutectoid Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\), which consists of \(\sim\)50 nm wide (Ni, Al)-rich B2 and (Fe, Mn)-rich fcc aligned phases, was annealed at 1173 K for various times up to 250 h in order to produce a wide range of phase widths (up to 2.5 \(\mu\)m) over which to evaluate the mechanical properties. The coarsening kinetics over these two orders of magnitude change could be described by \(\dot{\rho}^{2}-r_{\rm e}^{2}=kt\), where \(t\) is the phase width after time \(t\) at 1173 K. \(r_{\rm e}\) is initial phase width, and \(k\) is a constant. The yield strength, \(\sigma_{\rm p}\), was found to follow a Hall-Petch-type relationship, i.e. \(\sigma_{\rm p}-\sigma_{\rm o}+k_{\rm p,\dot{\rho}^{-1/2}}\), where \(\dot{\lambda}\) is the fcc phase width, \(\sigma_{\rm o}\) is the strength of the single-phase material, and \(k_{\rm p}\) is a constant. Interestingly, the work-hardening rate also roughly followed a \(\dot{\rho}^{-1/2}\) relationship, while the elongation to failure increased as \(\dot{\lambda}\) increased from failure before yielding in the as-cast material to \(\sim\)7% for the material with the largest \(\dot{\rho}\) values.

