## 1 Introduction

In earlier work, we showed, using a combination of scanning electron microscopy, transmission electron microscopy and atom probe tomography, that the as-cast microstructures of Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) and Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{26}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) (all compositions in at.%), consist of 65-85 nm wide (Ni, Al)-rich B2 (ordered bcc) and (Fe, Mn)-rich fcc aligned plates with a 50-100 nm wide fcc phase present along all the grain boundaries [1]. A few, very small (\(\sim\)5 nm) fcc precipitates with the same composition as the matrix fcc phase were present in the B2 phase. The two alloys, Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{26}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) and Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{28}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\), had high hardness values of 454 HV and 413 HV, respectively.

The idea of adding a ductile fcc phase to a stronger but brittle B2 phase is not new. It has been examined in the Ni-Al-Fe system [2; 3; 4], where the resulting multiphase alloys showed a good balance of strength and ductility. Similarly, the lamellar-structured fcc/B2 alloy Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{26}\)Mn\({}_{35}\)Al\({}_{15}\) also shows a good balance between room temperature strength and ductility [5; 6; 7; 8]. Thus, the presence of a fcc phase both in the matrix and along the grain boundaries in the FeNiMnAl alloys suggests that they may show some ductility even if the B2 phase is brittle.

In this paper, we show how the mechanical properties of the eutectoid alloy Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\), which has a similar microstructure to Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) and Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{25}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\), depend on the fcc phase width over a range of two orders of magnitude (produced by annealing at 1173 K). It is well-known that decreasing the lamellar spacing can substantially increase the yield strength, a feature demonstrated in duplex TiAl alloys [9; 10; 11; 12], pearlitic steels [13; 14; 15], two-phase Mg-Al-Cr-Ca alloys [16], and two-phase FeNiMnAl alloys [6; 17] among others. However, the effect of lamellae width on ductility is more nuanced with decreases in lamellar spacing producing increases in ductility in some studies [16; 18], reductions in ductility in some other studies [11], and having little effect on yet other studies [15]. In contrast, the effect of changes in lamellar spacing on the work hardening rate has rarely been reported.

## 2 Experimental

Ingots with an atomic composition of Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) were produced by arc melting under argon atmosphere pieces of 99.97% Fe, 99.95% Ni, 99.9% Mn, and 99.9% Al: an additional 5 wt.% of Mn was added to each melt since this element is often lost during melting due to its high vapor pressure. The ingots were melted three times and flipped over between each melting. After melting the ingots were allowed to cool in the water-cooled copper crucible of the arc melter resulting in grain sizes of \(\sim\)50 um. The compositions of the ingots, measured using a J601.850\(\rm\ddot{\rm{r}}\) electron probe microanalyzer, were found to be close to the target composition.

The as-cast material was annealed at 1173 K for various times from 0.25-250 h, in order to coarsen the microstructure, and cooled in air. The phases present and resulting microstructures were examined by X-ray diffraction (XRD), and by using a scanning electron microscope (SEM), and a transmission electron microscope (TEM) including energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). Details of specimen preparation and instrument operation can be found elsewhere [19].

Four types of mechanical testing were undertaken. Hardness tests were performed on the as-cast alloy using a TIME TH-713 Microhardness Tester with a load of 200 g (1.96 N) and a dwell time of 15 s.

The hardness of the individual phases after they had been coarsened after a 100 h anneal at 1173 K was determined using a Hysitron Ubi-1 nanoindenter using a procedure described elsewhere [6].

Room temperature tensile tests were performed on rectangular cross-section, dog-bone-shaped specimens with a gauge length of 10 mm, width of 2.54 mm and thickness of \(\sim\)1.27 mm at an initial strain rate of \(5\times 10^{-4}\) s\({}^{-1}\) on specimens after each anneal (three tests per annealing condition). The specimens were polished using silicon carbide papers and finished with 0.3 \(\mu\)m alumina powder to remove any surface cracks. The gauge length was measured before and after tensile testing using an optical microscope and the elongation to fracture determined from these measurements.

For the as-cast material, which showed fracture before yielding under tension, cuboidal (\(\sim\)3 \(\times\) 3 \(\times\) 8 mm) compression specimens were tested at an initial strain rate of \(5\times 10^{-4}\) s\({}^{-1}\), again with three specimens. These tests were stopped prior to fracture.

The fracture surfaces from the tensile tests were examined using secondary electron (SE) imaging on a FEI XL30 field emission gun scanning electron microscope.

## 3 Results

### As-cast microstructure

SE images of as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) are shown in Fig. 1 with the grain boundary (GB) labeled. The two-phase nature of the matrix with a fine, aligned microstructure is clearly evident. The grain boundary region has a different structure compared to the matrix, suggesting a different phase transformation process [1].

Fig. 2(a) shows a bright field (BF) TEM image of as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) taken along [100] of the B2 phase. The widths of the phases are \(\sim\)50 mm. Convergent beam electron diffraction patterns (CEDED) from the two phases showed that they were B2 and fcc, see Fig. 2(b) and (c). The BF TEM image in Fig. 2(d) shows the grain boundary phase and Fig. 2(e) is a CBED pattern showing that this phase is also fcc. In other words, the microstructure is very similar to that of the alloys Fe\({}_{30}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) and Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{28}\)Mn\({}_{30}\)Al\({}_{20}\) that were characterized in detail previously, although the phase widths are slightly smaller [1].

### Annealing behavior

The phases present and microstructure after annealing at 1173 K for various times up to 250 h followed air cooling were examined using XRD and a SEM. Fig. 3 shows XRD patterns of Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) after annealing at 1173 K for 1 h followed by quenching into water and after annealing at 1173 K for 20 h and air cooling. In both cases only B2 and fcc peaks are present.

Examination in the SEM showed that the fine aligned, two-phase microstructure of the as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\), see Fig. 4(a), had coarsed into a two-phase, lamellar structure that is no longer aligned even after only a anneal for 0.25 h at 1173 K, see Fig. 4(b). The grain boundary fcc is also no longer evident.

