which is also more brittle than the fcc phase [6]. In the present work, the elongation to failure increased almost linearly with increasing \(\dot{\lambda}\) up to \(\sim\)1 \(\upmu\)m after which it appeared to be largely independent of \(\dot{\lambda}\). Liu and Maziasz [33] found that the room-temperature elongation to failure of dual-phase TiAl alloys depended not on lamellar spacing but on the colony size. Hyzak and Bernstein [14] found that over the \(\dot{\lambda}\) range of 0.12-0.27 \(\upmu\)m, the ductility of pearlit steel was only very weakly dependent on \(\dot{\lambda}\), depending primarily on the prior austenite grain size, a result also found by Lewandowski and Thompson [45] in pearlittic steels. In contrast, Bae, Lee and Nam [36] reported that the reduction in area at the neck in tensile-tested pearlittic steels decreased with both increasing prior austenite grain size and lamellar spacing, although the lamellar spacing range studied was only 125-225 nm, and Dollar, Bernstein and Thompson [16] showed that for a fixed grain size of 30 \(\upmu\)m and fixed pearlit colony size of 8-10 \(\upmu\)m, the elongation to failure in pearlittic steel decreased from 51% to 27% with increasing lamellar spacing over a larger lamellar spacing range of 130-530 nm. In none of the above comparisons did the ductility increase with increasing lamellar spacing as observed in Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\).

## 5 Conclusions

The microstructure and mechanical properties of as-cast nanostructured eutectoid Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) were studied both before and after annealing at 1173 K for various times up to 250 h. It was found that:

1. The phase size increase from 50 nm to 2.5 \(\upmu\)m after annealing at 1173 K is well described by \(r^{2}-r_{o}{}^{2}=kt\), where \(r\) is the phase width after time \(t\), \(r_{o}\) is initial phase width, and \(k\) is a constant.
2. The yield strength, \(\sigma_{y}\), can be described, over two orders of magnitude range of \(\dot{\lambda}\), by the Hall-Petch-type relationship \(\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{o}+k_{y}\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\), where \(\dot{\lambda}\) is the fcc phase width, \(\sigma_{o}\) is the strength for the single phase material, and \(k_{y}\) is a constant.
3. The initial work-hardening rate followed a \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) relationship.
4. The elongation to failure increased almost linearly with \(\dot{\lambda}\) from fracture before yield in the as-cast state up to 7% at \(\dot{\lambda}\sim 1\) \(\upmu\)m, after which it appeared to be largely independent of \(\dot{\lambda}\).

